--TEST--
`Core\Out::capture` accepts exactly one of the answers a `through` callable can give and names what each of the others was — spec § 12, `rule:security/capture-answers-the-carrier` and `rule:types/anonymous-function`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// `rule:security/capture-answers-the-carrier` makes `capture` answer the sink's *carrier*, and § 12's
// `{through:}` a transform over it. A `callable` is opaque as to signature
// (`rule:types/anonymous-function`), so nothing static stands between that option and what the
// callable actually answers: this check is the whole of the guarantee behind
// the member's declared `Core\Cli\Text` return, and it is `Core\Out`'s only
// refusal.
final class Impostor {
    public function constructor(public string $name) {}
}

array<string> $labels = ["a"];

// Every shape a `through` can answer, the carrier first. A callable cannot be
// *called* through the variable holding it, but handing one to a `Core`
// member's `callable` option is the runtime's own call and does work — which
// is what lets this be a sweep rather than eight copies of one block.
array<callable> $filters = [
    fn (Core\Cli\Text $c): Core\Cli\Text => $c,
    fn (Core\Cli\Text $c): int => 7,
    fn (Core\Cli\Text $c): string => "s",
    fn (Core\Cli\Text $c): float => 1.5,
    fn (Core\Cli\Text $c): bool => true,
    fn (Core\Cli\Text $c): array<string> => $labels,
    fn (Core\Cli\Text $c): ?Core\Cli\Text => null,
    fn (Core\Cli\Text $c): Impostor => new Impostor("nope"),
];

// The claim is a partition, so it is counted rather than read off a line: of
// the eight, exactly the identity answers and the other seven refuse. Every
// refusal is wrapped in the member's own name, which is counted too — a
// message that grew its own opening would still look right on its own line.
// Nothing the refusing seven captured reaches this program's output either:
// the capture level is closed on both edges, so "written" appears once, from
// the one filter that answered a carrier for the `echo` to re-emit.
int $answered = 0;
int $refused = 0;
int $named = 0;
foreach ($filters as callable $filter) {
    try {
        echo Core\Out::capture(fn (): void => { echo "written"; }, {through: $filter});
        $answered = $answered + 1;
    } catch (Throwable $wrong) {
        $refused = $refused + 1;
        if (Core\Str::startsWith($wrong->message, '`Core\Out::capture`\'s `through` must answer a `Core\Cli\Text`, and this one answered ')) {
            $named = $named + 1;
        }
    }
}
echo "\n", $answered, "|", $refused, "|", $named, "\n";

// What each refusal names is what was actually answered, and the two kinds
// are named differently on purpose: a value that is not an object by its type,
// and an object by its class. Objecthood alone is not the test — a foreign
// object would otherwise be handed back under the member's declared
// `Core\Cli\Text`, a claim about the value that is not true, and the failure
// would surface wherever the carrier was next read rather than here.
try {
    echo Core\Out::capture(fn (): void => { echo "x"; }, {through: fn (Core\Cli\Text $c): int => 7});
} catch (Throwable $notAnObject) {
    echo $notAnObject->message, "\n";
}
try {
    echo Core\Out::capture(fn (): void => { echo "x"; }, {through: fn (Core\Cli\Text $c): Impostor => new Impostor("nope")});
} catch (Throwable $wrongClass) {
    echo $wrongClass->message, "\n";
}

// The sink below is untouched by any of it, and an omitted `through` is not a
// refused one — the option defaults to `Const::Null` rather than to an
// identity callable, and that arm never reaches the check at all.
echo Core\Out::capture(fn (): void => { echo "no filter"; }), "\n";
--EXPECT--
written
1|7|7
`Core\Out::capture`'s `through` must answer a `Core\Cli\Text`, and this one answered a value of type `int`
`Core\Out::capture`'s `through` must answer a `Core\Cli\Text`, and this one answered an instance of `Impostor`
no filter
